      "answer": "The customer whose issue started after the 2026-02-20 taxonomy rollout is BlueHarbor Logistics. Their issue was search relevance degradation in Event Nexus, with saved searches returning poor and duplicate results, increasing manual triage time to about 6 hours per day. This was triggered by the taxonomy rollout on 2026-02-20, as explicitly stated in the support ticket and retro analysis [art_8b0063fbb3cb, art_bd3560dfe194].\n\nThe proof plan proposed to BlueHarbor Logistics to get them comfortable with renewal includes:\n\n- A short-term fix involving updating index weighting and adding a translation/mapping layer to map new taxonomy terms to historical query tokens for the top 20 saved searches.\n- An A/B test in staging starting 2026-03-22 lasting 7-10 business days.\n- Validation metrics requiring top-5 correct hit rate >= 80% for prioritized queries, no regression in EN-RULES-ENGINE suppression logic, and incident correlation latency under 200ms.\n- A 90-day professional services engagement at $48k fixed price to implement the tuning and run the proof, with a 30% discount if Orchestrator seat expansion is signed concurrently.\n- A conditional credit clause in the SOW addendum that provides a one-quarter invoice credit if the proof does not meet the agreed accuracy uplift within 10 business days.\n- Medium-term fixes such as adding schema-to-index CI checks and a customer-configurable mapping UI.\n- Risk mitigation including fallback reindexing (~15 TB) with estimated 48 hours compute and $25k cost if mapping is insufficient.\n\nThis plan was detailed in the renewal negotiation call transcript and the retro analysis document, with agreed next steps including data sharing, staging environment setup, and SOW drafting by 2026-03-19 [art_0bccc580184e, art_bd3560dfe194].\n\nNo other customer's issue starting after the 2026-02-20 taxonomy rollout is documented in the provided evidence, so the answer is limited to BlueHarbor Logistics.",
